WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:01.840
I'm Sian from BBC Learning English,

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.980
and today we're going to look at the difference between

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.300
'lay' and 'lie'.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.220
So 'lay' always has an object,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.560
and it means

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.600
'put something or someone down carefully'

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.360
– normally in a flat position.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.720
When I eat I lay a cloth on the table.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.400
You can lay a baby in a cot.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.280
The past tense is 'laid',

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.460
but careful with the spelling.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.960
I laid all my cards on the table.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.200
The verb 'lie' doesn't have an object

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.040
and it means that you are 'in a flat position'

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.160
or you 'put yourself in a flat position'

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.920
- so you move on your own.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.960
Tonight I want to lie on the sofa and watch a film.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.380
But be careful.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.560
Now, the past of 'lie' is 'lay'.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.200
Yesterday I lay on the beach and read my book.

